meta {
  name: 13. Verify DELETE saga complete (dataset + sink gone)
  type: http
  seq: 23
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/datasets/:id
  body: none
  auth: inherit
}

params:path {
  id: {{pgDataSetId}}
}

script:pre-request {
  // Wait for the DATASET_DELETE saga (triggered in step 12) to finish. The teardown of the
  // NiFi flow, APISIX route/upstream and PostGIS sink runs asynchronously; the DataSet entity
  // is removed only once the saga completes. Poll GET /datasets/{id} until it returns 404.
  const axios = require('axios');
  const baseUrl = bru.getEnvVar('baseUrl');
  const keycloakUrl = bru.getEnvVar('keycloakUrl');
  const datasetId = bru.getVar('pgDataSetId');

  // Bruno's QuickJS sandbox has no URLSearchParams — build the form body by hand.
  const form = [
    ['grant_type', 'password'],
    ['client_id', bru.getEnvVar('clientId')],
    ['client_secret', bru.getEnvVar('clientSecret')],
    ['username', bru.getEnvVar('username')],
    ['password', bru.getEnvVar('password')],
  ].map(([k, v]) => `${encodeURIComponent(k)}=${encodeURIComponent(v)}`).join('&');

  // This is a wait-for-saga poll, not an assertion: a transient token or GET error must retry,
  // never abort the run. validateStatus keeps every response in-band; the tests block below is
  // what actually asserts the 404 end state.
  for (let i = 0; i < 30; i++) {
    try {
      const tokenResp = await axios.post(
        `${keycloakUrl}/realms/${bru.getEnvVar('realm')}/protocol/openid-connect/token`,
        form,
        {
          headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded' },
          validateStatus: () => true,
        }
      );
      const token = tokenResp.data && tokenResp.data.access_token;
      if (token) {
        const resp = await axios.get(
          `${baseUrl}/datasets/${datasetId}`,
          {
            headers: { 'Authorization': `Bearer ${token}` },
            validateStatus: () => true,
          }
        );
        if (resp.status === 404) {
          break;
        }
      }
    } catch (e) {
      // Ignore and retry — the assertion lives in the tests block.
    }
    await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 2000));
  }
}

tests {
  test("DataSet entity removed after DELETE saga", function() {
    // The entity is removed only once the DATASET_DELETE saga reports success, so a 404 here
    // proves the portal DataSet (and its cascaded sink + pipeline rows) is gone. It does NOT
    // observe the PostGIS DDL itself — the DROP runs in the config-adapter, out of reach of an
    // HTTP collection. A direct sink-table teardown assertion belongs in the E2E suite.
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(404);
  });
}

docs {
  **PostGIS saga step 13** — Verify the DATASET_DELETE saga completed.

  Polls `GET /datasets/{id}` (up to 60s) until it returns `404`. The DELETE saga triggered in step 12 de-provisions the NiFi flow, APISIX route/upstream and the PostGIS sink table, then removes the DataSet entity together with its sink and pipeline via cascade.

  **What the 404 proves — and what it does not.** The entity is removed only once the saga reports success, so the 404 establishes that the portal DataSet and its cascaded sink/pipeline rows are gone. It does **not** independently verify the data-bearing PostGIS table was dropped: that DDL runs inside the config-adapter, and this HTTP collection cannot query PostGIS to confirm it (Bruno's sandbox has only `axios`, no DB client). A premature or erroneous saga-completion result would let this step pass while leaving an orphan sink table behind. A true sink-teardown assertion (querying `information_schema` after the delete) belongs in the shell-based E2E suite, not here.

  There is no separate manual sink delete anymore: the saga owns the sink teardown.

  **Expected Response:** 404 Not Found (dataset entity removed after the DELETE saga).
}
